  "public_messages": [
    "I\u2019ll read the local brief, source, memory instructions, and the requested OpenSocrates guidance, then implement and verify the API within this workspace.",
    "The workspace contains the supplied SQLite driver helper and test, with no server implementation yet. I\u2019ll preserve those files and use SQLite transactions for claim and idempotency coordination across processes.",
    "The store now uses `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` for mutations, so SQLite serializes claims and idempotent submissions across server processes. Next I\u2019m wiring strict request validation and the HTTP routes, then I\u2019ll run behavioral tests.",
    "The focused Go tests pass for concurrent duplicate submissions, shared SQLite connections, tenant isolation, lease deadlines and fencing, heartbeat, batch validation, max attempts, and a reopen of the database. I\u2019m tightening a few request edge cases and running a separate two-process restart check.",
    "The two-process check passed: one process replayed a job created by the other, claimed it, and a restarted process read the acknowledged lease after both earlier processes were killed. I\u2019m recording a bounded continuity checkpoint and checking the final diff.",
    "Implemented the QueueForge backend API, SQLite store, server CLI, focused tests, and [README](<WORKSPACE>/README.md). The supplied driver files and `go.mod`/`go.sum` were unchanged. No commit was made.\n\nVerification passed with Go 1.26.3: `go build -o bin/queueforge ./cmd/server`, `go test ./...`, and `go vet ./...`. Tests cover concurrent idempotency, simultaneous claims, tenant isolation, lease fencing and deadline equality, heartbeat, max attempts, batch validation, and database reopen. A separate two-process check confirmed shared-database replay and claim, then read the acknowledged lease after SIGKILL and restart. All test processes were stopped.\n\nLimits: there is no job retention or idempotency-key expiry. Tenant headers are synthetic identity; the service provides no real authentication or TLS."
  ],
